1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:00,920
Right now,

2
00:00:00,920 --> 00:00:06,312
a wind from the Sun is blowing past Earth at around four hundred kilometers per second.

3
00:00:06,312 --> 00:00:10,217
Fast enough to cross the United States in about eleven seconds.

4
00:00:10,717 --> 00:00:11,712
You never feel it,

5
00:00:11,712 --> 00:00:15,580
because of an invisible shield made in the molten heart of the planet.

6
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:17,660
What if that shield switched off?

7
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:23,920
Here's where the shield comes from.

8
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:27,325
About two thousand nine hundred kilometers below your feet,

9
00:00:27,325 --> 00:00:31,308
there's an ocean of liquid iron and nickel, thousands of degrees hot.

10
00:00:31,808 --> 00:00:36,796
As it churns and swirls, it works like a giant electric generator, a dynamo.

11
00:00:36,796 --> 00:00:39,290
And that makes Earth's magnetic field.

12
00:00:39,290 --> 00:00:44,410
Down in the core, the field is about fifty times stronger than at the surface.

13
00:00:45,267 --> 00:00:48,164
At the surface, the field is surprisingly weak.

14
00:00:48,164 --> 00:00:51,061
A fridge magnet is about twenty times stronger.

15
00:00:51,561 --> 00:00:52,785
But it's enormous.

16
00:00:52,785 --> 00:00:54,622
On the side facing the Sun,

17
00:00:54,622 --> 00:00:58,364
it holds the solar wind back about ten Earth radii out,

18
00:00:58,364 --> 00:01:01,153
more than sixty thousand kilometers away.

19
00:01:01,653 --> 00:01:06,353
On the night side, it's stretched into a long tail that reaches far past the Moon.

20
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:08,789
So here are the rules.

21
00:01:08,789 --> 00:01:12,255
In one instant, the dynamo stops making a field.

22
00:01:12,255 --> 00:01:13,049
Completely.

23
00:01:13,049 --> 00:01:14,422
The core stays hot.

24
00:01:14,422 --> 00:01:16,299
The planet keeps spinning.

25
00:01:16,299 --> 00:01:18,249
Only the magnetism is gone.

26
00:01:18,749 --> 00:01:20,429
This can't actually happen.

27
00:01:20,429 --> 00:01:27,147
Geophysicists think the core has enough energy to keep the dynamo running for hundreds of millions of years,

28
00:01:27,147 --> 00:01:28,267
possibly billions.

29
00:01:28,267 --> 00:01:30,195
But let's switch it off anyway.

30
00:01:32,933 --> 00:01:35,662
In the first second, nothing you can feel.

31
00:01:35,662 --> 00:01:36,247
No sound.

32
00:01:36,247 --> 00:01:36,831
No flash.

33
00:01:37,331 --> 00:01:40,392
But every compass on Earth just stopped pointing north.

34
00:01:40,392 --> 00:01:43,563
The needles drift, and settle wherever they happen to be.

35
00:01:44,063 --> 00:01:49,559
Magnetic north has wandered more than eleven hundred kilometers since 1831.

36
00:01:49,559 --> 00:01:50,879
In recent decades,

37
00:01:50,879 --> 00:01:55,788
it was racing toward Siberia at up to fifty-five kilometers a year.

38
00:01:55,788 --> 00:01:57,840
Now it simply doesn't exist.

39
00:01:58,340 --> 00:02:00,681
Your phone's compass gives up too.

40
00:02:00,681 --> 00:02:06,395
GPS keeps working, though, because it runs on satellites and clocks, not magnetism.

41
00:02:06,395 --> 00:02:06,945
For now.

42
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:11,973
Within minutes, the solar wind arrives.

43
00:02:11,973 --> 00:02:16,576
With nothing to hold it back, it slams straight into the top of the atmosphere.

44
00:02:17,076 --> 00:02:18,423
That sounds terrifying.

45
00:02:18,423 --> 00:02:20,181
But here's the first surprise:

46
00:02:20,181 --> 00:02:22,465
down on the ground, you're mostly fine.

47
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:27,780
What really shields you from space radiation isn't the magnetic field.

48
00:02:27,780 --> 00:02:28,612
It's the air.

49
00:02:28,612 --> 00:02:33,668
Above every square centimeter of your body sits about a kilogram of atmosphere.

50
00:02:34,168 --> 00:02:38,534
That's roughly as much shielding as ten meters of water over your head.

51
00:02:38,534 --> 00:02:38,903
Today,

52
00:02:38,903 --> 00:02:43,884
cosmic rays give you only about a third of a millisievert a year at ground level,

53
00:02:43,884 --> 00:02:47,082
around a tenth of your natural background radiation.

54
00:02:47,582 --> 00:02:54,778
One model of a much weaker field found about thirteen percent more cosmic-ray ionization at ground level worldwide,

55
00:02:54,778 --> 00:02:57,406
and about a quarter more near the equator.

56
00:02:57,406 --> 00:03:01,035
High in the atmosphere, the jump was several times bigger.

57
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:06,106
By that night, the sky looks different.

58
00:03:06,106 --> 00:03:10,778
Auroras normally hug the poles, because the field funnels particles down there.

59
00:03:10,778 --> 00:03:12,966
Without the field, there's no funnel.

60
00:03:13,466 --> 00:03:16,193
Mars has no global magnetic field today,

61
00:03:16,193 --> 00:03:20,830
and during big solar storms its whole sky can light up with a faint,

62
00:03:20,830 --> 00:03:22,125
planet-wide aurora.

63
00:03:22,625 --> 00:03:25,110
Earth could get something similar.

64
00:03:25,110 --> 00:03:28,838
Dim glows over every latitude, not just the Arctic.

65
00:03:28,838 --> 00:03:32,565
Even today, a big storm can push auroras far south.

66
00:03:32,565 --> 00:03:36,293
In May 2024, they were seen as far south as Mexico.

67
00:03:37,133 --> 00:03:39,480
Up in orbit, it's a different story.

68
00:03:39,480 --> 00:03:44,761
The Van Allen radiation belts are held in place by the field, so they drain away.

69
00:03:44,761 --> 00:03:45,869
That sounds good.

70
00:03:46,369 --> 00:03:51,414
But now satellites are directly exposed to every blast of particles from the Sun.

71
00:03:51,414 --> 00:03:54,590
Astronauts lose a big part of their protection too.

72
00:03:55,090 --> 00:03:57,125
Even airline crews would notice.

73
00:03:57,125 --> 00:04:03,038
A 2026 study found that if Earth's field were as weak as it was forty-one thousand years ago,

74
00:04:03,038 --> 00:04:06,980
radiation on some flight routes would be significantly higher.

75
00:04:09,733 --> 00:04:12,248
Within a year, the Sun throws a big storm.

76
00:04:12,248 --> 00:04:13,386
It does that a lot.

77
00:04:13,886 --> 00:04:19,302
In 1859, a solar storm called the Carrington Event made telegraph lines spark.

78
00:04:19,302 --> 00:04:24,023
Some operators kept sending messages with their batteries unplugged,

79
00:04:24,023 --> 00:04:26,731
running only on current from the storm.

80
00:04:27,231 --> 00:04:27,837
In 1989,

81
00:04:27,837 --> 00:04:33,897
a storm knocked out power to six million people in Quebec within ninety seconds,

82
00:04:33,897 --> 00:04:35,034
for nine hours.

83
00:04:35,034 --> 00:04:35,943
And in 2022,

84
00:04:35,943 --> 00:04:41,397
a minor storm dragged thirty-eight new Starlink satellites out of orbit.

85
00:04:41,397 --> 00:04:46,320
The 2024 storm even sent GPS-guided tractors drifting off course.

86
00:04:46,820 --> 00:04:48,611
All of that happened with the shield up.

87
00:04:49,467 --> 00:04:53,398
Without it, particles from big storms pour into the upper atmosphere,

88
00:04:53,398 --> 00:04:55,848
where they create chemicals that eat ozone.

89
00:04:56,348 --> 00:05:02,390
A 2024 study modeled an extreme solar storm hitting an Earth with no magnetic field.

90
00:05:02,390 --> 00:05:06,418
Severe ozone loss lasted for years instead of about one,

91
00:05:06,418 --> 00:05:11,238
and ultraviolet light at the surface rose by up to about a quarter.

92
00:05:11,738 --> 00:05:13,497
More UV means faster sunburns,

93
00:05:13,497 --> 00:05:17,369
and more stress for plants and plankton that live in the sunlight.

94
00:05:18,233 --> 00:05:21,735
Meanwhile, animals that navigate by magnetism get lost.

95
00:05:21,735 --> 00:05:27,084
Sea turtles seem to remember the magnetic signature of the beach where they hatched,

96
00:05:27,084 --> 00:05:29,440
and return to it years later to nest.

97
00:05:29,940 --> 00:05:33,853
Salmon use it to find their way home from the open ocean.

98
00:05:33,853 --> 00:05:39,208
Many migrating birds, like European robins, carry a built-in magnetic compass.

99
00:05:39,708 --> 00:05:40,879
Most have backups:

100
00:05:40,879 --> 00:05:42,570
the Sun, the stars, smell.

101
00:05:42,570 --> 00:05:45,171
But a lot of migrations would get messy.

102
00:05:47,933 --> 00:05:49,238
Now the big question.

103
00:05:49,238 --> 00:05:54,272
Without a shield, does the solar wind strip away our air, the way it did on Mars?

104
00:05:54,772 --> 00:05:59,059
Mars lost its global magnetic field about four billion years ago.

105
00:05:59,059 --> 00:06:04,269
NASA's MAVEN spacecraft found the solar wind is still stripping its atmosphere,

106
00:06:04,269 --> 00:06:06,644
around a hundred grams every second.

107
00:06:06,644 --> 00:06:10,996
Over billions of years, most of the Martian air was lost to space.

108
00:06:10,996 --> 00:06:15,811
All that's left of its field are patches of magnetized rock in the crust,

109
00:06:15,811 --> 00:06:17,789
fossils of a dynamo that died.

110
00:06:18,633 --> 00:06:20,285
But here's the surprise.

111
00:06:20,285 --> 00:06:23,451
Venus has no magnetic field of its own either.

112
00:06:23,451 --> 00:06:27,511
And its atmosphere is about ninety times heavier than ours.

113
00:06:27,511 --> 00:06:33,017
Instead, the solar wind's own magnetism piles up around Venus into a weak shell.

114
00:06:33,517 --> 00:06:39,384
Some researchers argue a magnetic field doesn't protect air as much as we assumed.

115
00:06:39,384 --> 00:06:41,817
A 2018 study estimated that Earth,

116
00:06:41,817 --> 00:06:45,251
Mars and Venus all lose roughly similar amounts,

117
00:06:45,251 --> 00:06:49,043
around half a kilogram to two kilograms every second.

118
00:06:49,543 --> 00:06:50,316
At that pace,

119
00:06:50,316 --> 00:06:56,561
a planet as big as Earth would take far longer than a million years to lose any meaningful amount of air.

120
00:06:56,561 --> 00:06:59,297
So a million years in, you're still breathing.

121
00:07:00,133 --> 00:07:02,199
And this isn't purely hypothetical.

122
00:07:02,699 --> 00:07:04,946
About forty-one thousand years ago,

123
00:07:04,946 --> 00:07:07,898
during an event called the Laschamp excursion,

124
00:07:07,898 --> 00:07:12,198
Earth's field collapsed to around five percent of today's strength.

125
00:07:12,198 --> 00:07:13,995
The magnetic poles wandered.

126
00:07:13,995 --> 00:07:15,920
Neanderthals were still alive.

127
00:07:16,420 --> 00:07:20,072
Some scientists, studying ancient New Zealand kauri trees,

128
00:07:20,072 --> 00:07:22,465
argued the ozone layer took a beating,

129
00:07:22,465 --> 00:07:26,242
and linked the event to climate shifts and even extinctions.

130
00:07:26,742 --> 00:07:29,010
Other researchers strongly disagree.

131
00:07:29,010 --> 00:07:32,160
They see no clear climate signal in the ice cores.

132
00:07:32,160 --> 00:07:33,798
The debate is still going.

133
00:07:34,633 --> 00:07:35,673
And the field today?

134
00:07:35,673 --> 00:07:39,050
It's about nine percent weaker than it was two hundred years ago.

135
00:07:39,550 --> 00:07:42,028
A weak spot over the South Atlantic,

136
00:07:42,028 --> 00:07:44,643
where satellites take extra radiation,

137
00:07:44,643 --> 00:07:49,805
has grown since 2014 by an area nearly half the size of continental Europe.

138
00:07:50,305 --> 00:07:55,018
But the European Space Agency says that's still within normal ups and downs.

139
00:07:55,018 --> 00:07:58,490
The field is still about twice its million-year average.

140
00:07:59,333 --> 00:08:01,043
So the shield won't switch off.

141
00:08:01,043 --> 00:08:04,020
But it flickers, it wanders, and one day it will flip.

142
00:08:04,520 --> 00:08:08,775
If losing it meant auroras over your city every night, would you switch it off?

143
00:08:08,775 --> 00:08:10,067
Tell me in the comments.
